# lazily-stays-on-its-element: `lazily` swaps only the splits of its
# element's own repetition, and doesn't reach the elements inside a nested
# `<…>`. In `one or more of <digits> lazily` the outer repetition prefers
# one pass, but the `digits` inside stays greedy, so it matches "#123".
# `digits lazily`, whose own repetition is lazy, matches "#1" (spec chapter
# 8, Compiling). The matches are pinned. Its Fuel, allocation and state
# figures are Cost Model 0's, as the TS Core blessed them.

> load search identity=983af1d5fd62ac4523e68f3d85643b61ca5937b61354cd1a2f31b7650f0c97f5

# The first program is 0 char "#", 1 class digit, 2 split 1 3 (the greedy
# `digits`), 3 split 4 1 (the lazy outer repetition), 4 match. After each
# digit the thread list is [class digit, match], so the greedy thread comes
# first, and the match at the end is the one kept. 11 steps, and 9 for the
# second Match Search.
> deliver d1 to=search message=go
> pump clock=2026-09-30T09:00:00Z
seg search/r1 start delivery=d1 handler=go clause=1 fuel=47 alloc=516 state=516 end=return
run search/r1 outcome=completed delivery=d1 handler=go fuel=47 alloc=516
pumped state=idle fuel=47

> vars
vars search outer=[{text: "#123", range: 1..4, captures: {}, ranges: {}}] plain=[{text: "#1", range: 1..2, captures: {}, ranges: {}}]
